#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120656} :activity/id #uuid "b4e56add-13ad-5fc4-ab0a-d5396c88286c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051083 :topic/id #uuid "7fcb6c0b-c77e-5abe-8b04-125d08ffe36e" :topic/math-academy-id 1780} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121029 :tutorial/content "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f8c6994-f448-5679-8d2e-5591b86c2a97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3178 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d1731663-b6e6-5bf1-a62a-a1b6ab66b554" :step/math-academy-id 17360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119605 :question/id #uuid "f7324b66-d8f7-5bdb-9786-4160808f2a7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4768"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048947}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557314}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"} :step/id #uuid "02b00fc5-d232-5394-bd52-5c011f1db096" :step/math-academy-id 17361 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119606 :question/id #uuid "58955d86-8a94-5174-83fa-0723